# What each halftone type is, how it is screened, and where this
# interpreter and the specification differ about it.
#
# PLRM Table 7.3 names ten types of halftone dictionary. They are not
# alike: some carry a spot function, some a threshold array, one is a
# composite keyed by colorant, two describe a cell that sits at an
# angle, and two carry no screen at all. A program meets those
# differences directly -- a type that answers rangecheck is a type it
# cannot use -- and a feature given to one of them is a question that
# has to be put to the rest.
#
# Membership is DERIVED. The guard asks the interpreter about every type
# code from 0 to 100 and calls a type SCREENED when sethalftone answers
# anything but rangecheck, so a type newly implemented shows up here
# whether or not anyone remembered to say so, and one that quietly stops
# working shows up too. The register is held to that answer in both
# directions.
#
# Each line is
#
#   <type>  <screens|refused>  <lattice>  <normaliser>  <component>  <reason>
#
# lattice     where the cell geometry comes from. spot: searched for
#             from a frequency and an angle. sides: stated outright.
#             parts: the components carry their own. none: no cell.
#             Held to the source: whatever the value, the determinant
#             and the tile side must come from the one derivation, so a
#             second parameterisation cannot be written without saying
#             so here.
# normaliser  which helper presents this type as a simpler one, or none.
#             DERIVED from the router in data/halftone.ps and held to this
#             column both ways: a type that acquires a helper of its own
#             fails here rather than passing unexamined.
# component   whether PLRM 7.4.6 allows the type inside a type 5
#             dictionary -- may, or maynot. Probed against the
#             interpreter; a disagreement must appear in the divergences
#             below or the guard fails.
# reason      required. For a screened type, what the type is; for a
#             refused one, why there is nothing to screen with.

1    screens  spot   none        may     a single screen given as frequency, angle and spot function, PLRM Table 7.4
2    screens  spot   graytriple  maynot  four screens, one per primary, each a frequency, angle and spot function; this interpreter screens grey, so the grey triple is the one that reaches a device
3    screens  sides  none        may     a single screen given as an 8-bit threshold array in a string, PLRM Table 7.5
4    screens  sides  graytriple  maynot  four threshold screens, one per primary; the grey one reaches the device as in a type 2
5    screens  parts  component   maynot  a composite keyed by colorant name, whose components are dictionaries of other types, PLRM 7.4.6
6    screens  sides  none        may     a type 3 whose threshold array arrives through a file rather than a string, PLRM Table 7.6
9    refused  none   none        may     a proprietary halftone (PLRM 7.4.7) carrying no screen: only the type and perhaps a name. The screen lives in a device built for the technique, and no device here has one
10   screens  sides  angledcell  may     a cell at a nonzero screen angle, given as the two squares it divides into, PLRM Table 7.7
16   screens  sides  angledcell  may     a type 6 at two bytes a threshold, optionally with the second rectangle that makes it an angled cell, PLRM Table 7.8
100  refused  none   none        may     a proprietary halftone as type 9 is, able to carry further entries private to one product. Same reason, same absence of a device with the technique

entries 10

# Every entry name the extent rule reaches, and what it is.
#
# A cell of no extent is refused, and the rule that does it matches an
# entry by the END of its name so that the copies a type 2 makes for
# each primary, and the second rectangle of an angled cell, are covered
# without any of them being listed. Matching by ending reaches names
# nobody thought about, which is the point of it and also its hazard:
# ActualFrequency ends in Frequency and is not an extent at all but an
# answer the operator writes, and it was refused for weeks of nothing
# noticing because every test of the rule asked whether it REFUSED and
# none asked what it had stopped ACCEPTING.
#
# So the reach is written down. Every name in PLRM's halftone tables
# that ends in a word the rule matches is here, with what it is, and the
# guard offers each one to sethalftone at nought and requires the
# verdict this says. A suffix added to the rule, or an entry the
# specification gains, changes what is reached and fails here until
# somebody says which of the two it is.
#
#   entry <name>  <extent|answer>  <what it is>
#
# extent  a size the program states; nought or less describes no area
# answer  a value the operator writes; what the program put there is
#         not a statement about anything and must not be read as one

entry Frequency       extent  the screen frequency of a type 1, PLRM Table 7.4
entry Width           extent  the width of a threshold cell, PLRM Table 7.5
entry Height          extent  the height of a threshold cell, PLRM Table 7.5
entry Width2          extent  the width of an angled cell's second rectangle, PLRM Table 7.8
entry Height2         extent  the height of that second rectangle
entry Xsquare         extent  the side of a type 10's first square, PLRM Table 7.7
entry Ysquare         extent  the side of its second square
entry RedFrequency    extent  a type 2's red screen frequency, PLRM 7.4.4
entry GreenFrequency  extent  a type 2's green screen frequency
entry BlueFrequency   extent  a type 2's blue screen frequency
entry GrayFrequency   extent  a type 2's grey screen frequency, the one that reaches a device here
entry RedWidth        extent  a type 4's red cell width, PLRM 7.4.4
entry RedHeight       extent  a type 4's red cell height
entry GreenWidth      extent  a type 4's green cell width
entry GreenHeight     extent  a type 4's green cell height
entry BlueWidth       extent  a type 4's blue cell width
entry BlueHeight      extent  a type 4's blue cell height
entry GrayWidth       extent  a type 4's grey cell width
entry GrayHeight      extent  a type 4's grey cell height
entry ActualFrequency answer  what sethalftone sets to the frequency achieved, PLRM Table 7.4. The program supplies the slot and reads it back, so its incoming value states nothing

entries-reached 20

# The entries PLRM types that no halftone type REQUIRES, and the types
# each may hold. These are the ones a program can misspell with no
# consequence: nothing here needs them to screen, so a value of the wrong
# type would otherwise be accepted and handed back by currenthalftone as
# though it had been checked.
#
# Held to the interpreter's own table in both directions, so an entry
# added there without a line here fails, and a line naming an entry the
# table does not carry fails too. The types are the ones PLRM's tables
# give, not the ones an implementation happens to accept.
#
#   optional <name>  <type>[,<type>...]  <what it is>

optional AccurateScreens  booleantype           the flag asking for the precise screening algorithm, PLRM Table 7.4. Available in types 1 and 2; typed wherever it appears, because PLRM reserves the name across the family and a type that gains the entry should not have to gain the check with it
optional HalftoneName     nametype,stringtype   the name of the halftone dictionary, PLRM Tables 7.4 to 7.9. Read by the colour rendering dictionary selection, which forms the name intent.deviceconfig.halftone from it

optionals 2

# Where this interpreter and the specification differ, and what is to
# become of each difference. A difference the guard can see and this
# list does not name is a failure; so is a line here naming a difference
# the guard can no longer find, because a line that has outlived its
# defect reads exactly like one that still holds.
#
#   <what>  <settled|thorn|heading>  <reason>
#
# settled  the difference is deliberate and is not going to change
# thorn    a known defect or gap, waiting on work
# heading  being changed now


transfer-required  settled  PLRM Table 7.4 says a TransferFunction is REQUIRED of a type 5 component standing for a nonprimary colorant or a component of a DeviceN space. Neither implementation this is adjudicated against enforces that: both accept such a component without one, as this interpreter does. Refusing it would turn away programs both of them run, so the letter is not followed here and the reason is that following it would be the divergence
one-screening-algorithm  settled  PLRM 7.4.4 has an AccurateScreens of true substitute a highly precise halftoning algorithm for the ordinary one. There is one screening algorithm here, so a true value leaves ordinary halftoning in force, which is what the specification says happens when the entry is false or absent. MEASURED as the same achieved screen either way. What makes this settled rather than a defect is that nothing lies about it: PLRM's own account of the feature is that it achieves the requested frequency and angle with higher accuracy, and sethalftone writes the frequency and angle it ACTUALLY achieved into ActualFrequency and ActualAngle whenever the program supplies those slots (verified: a requested 60 at 30 degrees reports what the quantised cell gives). A program that needs high accuracy asks for it, is given the ordinary screen, and can read that it did not get what it asked for -- which is the whole of what the entry is for. The second algorithm is a screening-geometry project for high-resolution separation work and is not on the way; the entry is validated, kept, and reported

divergences 2
